## std/ffi/ptr — the C-pointer ⇆ Option boundary (B.15b)
##
## The sanctioned bridge between raw, nullable C pointers (`CPtr`) and
## Quartz's typed absence (`Option`). This is the FFI edge described in
## `docs/design/B15_NIL_ELIMINATION.md §5` — the one place a value's
## nullability cannot be proven (a C function may return NULL), handled
## the way Kotlin handles platform types: defer at the boundary, check on
## entry, and route every NULL through an explicit `Option` rather than
## letting a bare `0`/`nil` leak into typed Quartz code.
##
## `CPtr` itself is never niched (a NULL pointer is a legitimate FFI
## value), so `Option<CPtr>` carries an explicit tag — `Some(p)` always
## holds a *non-null* pointer, `None` means the C call returned NULL.
##
## Usage:
##   import std/ffi/ptr
##
##   raw = some_c_call()                 # extern "C" def -> CPtr (maybe NULL)
##   match cptr_to_option(raw)
##     Some(p) => use_pointer(p)         # p guaranteed non-null
##     None    => handle_absence()       # C returned NULL
##   end

## Forward bridge: a possibly-NULL C pointer → `Option<CPtr>`.
##
## `None` if the pointer is NULL; `Some(p)` (a guaranteed-non-null
## pointer) otherwise. Turns a C "NULL means absent/error" contract into a
## typed `Option` that callers must handle.
def cptr_to_option(p: CPtr): Option<CPtr>
  if cptr_to_int(p) == 0
    return Option::None
  end
  return Option::Some(p)
end

## Reverse bridge: an `Option<CPtr>` → a raw C pointer (`None` → NULL).
##
## For passing an optional pointer back across the FFI boundary to a C
## function that accepts NULL. This is also the *explicit-unsafe* unwrap
## (B.15 Q2's `cptr_unwrap_unchecked`): for `Option<CPtr>` there is no
## separate `NonNull` newtype, so "extract the pointer, treating absence
## as NULL" and "unchecked unwrap" are the same operation — None yields
## the NULL pointer rather than trapping. Use `.unwrap()` instead when a
## `None` should be a loud panic.
def cptr_from_option(opt: Option<CPtr>): CPtr
  match opt
    Some(p) => return p
    None => return int_to_cptr(0)
  end
end
